--- "wierd-looking jello effect for advisor shield."
declare MAT_WARPSHIELD: number
--- **WARNING**: The primitive type `MATERIAL_POINTS` does not currently work and will not produce any visual effect if used. For more information, see `Point Primitives`
declare MATERIAL_POINTS: number
--- Creates line using 2 vertices. The `primitiveCount` argument of `mesh.Begin` should be the total number of line multiply by 2 that the Mesh will contain. E.g. `(vertexCount * 2)` For more information, see `Line Primitives`
declare MATERIAL_LINES: number
--- Creates triangles from groupings of 3 vertices. The `primitiveCount` argument of `mesh.Begin` should be the total number of triangles that the Mesh will contain. E.g. `(vertexCount / 3)` For more information, see `Triangle Primitives`
declare MATERIAL_TRIANGLES: number
--- Creates a set of triangles that each share two vertices with the previous triangle in the sequence. The `primitiveCount` argument of `mesh.Begin` should be the total number of triangles that the Mesh will contain. E.g. `(vertexCount - 2)` For more information, see `Triangle Strip Primitives`
declare MATERIAL_TRIANGLE_STRIP: number
--- Creates a set of lines that all share a single vertex and each share a vertex with the previous lines. The `primitiveCount` argument of `mesh.Begin` should be the total number of line connection that the Mesh will contain. E.g. `(vertexCount)` For more information, see `Line Strip Primitives`
declare MATERIAL_LINE_STRIP: number
--- Exactly like MATERIAL_LINE_STRIP do, but the first and the last vertex will always be connected together. For more information, see `Line Loop Primitives`
declare MATERIAL_LINE_LOOP: number
--- Creates a set of triangles that all share a single vertex and each share a vertex with the previous triangle. The `primitiveCount` argument of `mesh.Begin` should be the total number of vertices that the Mesh will contain. E.g. `(vertexCount)` For more information, see `Polygon Primitives`
declare MATERIAL_POLYGON: number
--- Creates pairs of triangles that share two vertices. The `primitiveCount` argument of `mesh.Begin` should be the total number of quads that the Mesh will contain. E.g. `(vertexCount / 4)` For more information, see `Quad Primitives`
declare MATERIAL_QUADS: number
--- Cull back faces with counterclockwise vertices.
declare MATERIAL_CULLMODE_CCW: number
--- Cull back faces with clockwise vertices.
declare MATERIAL_CULLMODE_CW: number
--- Do not cull back faces at all.
declare MATERIAL_CULLMODE_NONE: number
--- No fog
declare MATERIAL_FOG_NONE: number
--- Linear fog
declare MATERIAL_FOG_LINEAR: number
--- For use in conjunction with `render.SetFogZ`. Does not work if start distance is bigger than end distance. Ignores density setting. Seems to be broken? Used for underwater fog by the engine.
declare MATERIAL_FOG_LINEAR_BELOW_FOG_Z: number
--- No light
declare MATERIAL_LIGHT_DISABLE: number
--- Point light
declare MATERIAL_LIGHT_POINT: number
--- Directional light
declare MATERIAL_LIGHT_DIRECTIONAL: number
--- Spot light
declare MATERIAL_LIGHT_SPOT: number
--- Do not create a depth-stencil buffer.Use the default depth-stencil buffer if used as render target 0.
declare MATERIAL_RT_DEPTH_SHARED: number
--- Create a depth-stencil buffer.Use the created depth-stencil buffer if used as render target 0.
declare MATERIAL_RT_DEPTH_SEPARATE: number
--- Do not create a depth-stencil buffer.Disable depth and stencil buffer usage if used as render target 0.
declare MATERIAL_RT_DEPTH_NONE: number
--- Create a depth-stencil buffer.Use the created depth-stencil buffer if used as render target 0. Creates a color texture despite the name. Seems to behave the same as MATERIAL_RT_DEPTH_SEPARATE.
declare MATERIAL_RT_DEPTH_ONLY: number
